@vsm 1.0
@status active
// s1_isolation_relay_arch_2026-08-02.vsm
// Author: Claude [O] + rmw3 (S5), 2026-08-02.
// S5-ratified architecture arc, registered BEFORE dispatch (spec-first pattern, same discipline as
// attack_surface_expansion.spec.vsm and dynamic_resources_all_lxc.spec.vsm). Grew out of the jupyter-lab
// wiring question (is dev-jupyter/114 coupled to the nucleus? No — verified) which widened into: what IS
// the general policy for S1 isolation, config-canon integrity, and resource-pool scope across ALL LXCs.
// Cross-references 3 EXISTING specs rather than duplicating them — read those for prior ratified detail:
//   - docs/spec/relay/lxc105_formal_study_2026-07-31.vsm  (105's original SOC/BBH relay question)
//   - docs/spec/dynamic_resources_all_lxc.spec.vsm         (TIER1/TIER2 resource model, ratified 07-03)
//   - docs/spec/nats_auth_remediation/spec.vsm             (hardcoded-URL auth-mute bug class, 07-24)

spec(S1-ISOLATION-RELAY-ARCH) {

  status: "RATIFIED (architecture direction) — NOT dispatched. rmw3 2026-08-02: 'quedamos a la espera de
      que DS termine su tarea' (SOC-STAGE-A-PHASE1-WAZUH-MINIMAL-INSTALL) before any of this ships.
      This doc exists so the arc is fully specified when DS frees up — nothing here is speculative
      framing, every decision below was explicitly ratified in conversation before being written down.",

  // ── DECISION 1: universal VLAN-per-S1, no exceptions ──────────────────────────────
  decision_1_universal_vlan_isolation: {
    ratified: "rmw3: 'sobre si las VLANs deberian ser universales, yo diria que si, como política sin
        excepción, aun que signifique recablear clima y econ.' No grandfathering.",
    concrete_implication: "s1-clima (112, 10.10.20.112) and s1-econ (113, 10.10.20.113) currently share
        VLAN20 with the nucleus itself (101, 10.10.20.10) — verified live via state.db lxcs + coupling_registry
        today. Under this policy they get recabled to their own VLAN(s), same pattern as jupyter-lab's
        VLAN40 build (dedicated tag, no firewall=1 on net0 to avoid the fwbr/MASQUERADE egress bug already
        root-caused 2026-08-01, host-level FORWARD DROP toward VLAN20/nucleus).",
    open_question_not_decided: "One VLAN per S1 individually, or grouped by isolation TIER (e.g. clima+econ
        — both internal-domain, no external-hostile contact — could plausibly share a lighter tier/VLAN,
        distinct from bbh's adversarial tier)? rmw3's 'sin excepción' settles THAT every S1 must be isolated
        from the nucleus's own VLAN — it does not yet settle whether same-tier S1s can share a VLAN with
        EACH OTHER. Leave as an explicit open question for the relay design session, not assumed either way.",
  },

  // ── DECISION 2: relay = one per isolation tier, not global, not per-S1 ────────────
  decision_2_relay_granularity: {
    ratified: "rmw3: '105 va a necesitar cambiar su nombre a s1-relay/s1-proxy... puede existir un solo
        relay gestionando todos los s1, o relays especificos dependiendo del grado de aislamiento.' Claude's
        recommendation (one relay per isolation tier/VLAN, not global-single, not per-individual-S1) —
        reasoning: a global relay spanning clima (low-risk internal) through bbh (high-risk, touches hostile
        external targets by design) makes a single relay compromise cascade across incompatible risk tiers;
        per-individual-S1 relays over-provision as the system scales toward the 'hundreds of containers'
        target rmw3 named earlier this session. rmw3 accepted this framing ('se alinea con lo que vengo
        observando') without further amendment.",
    concrete_step_1: "105 (currently 's1-bbh', declared-but-never-integrated bbh-prod placeholder, R6-gated)
        is repurposed, not demolished: becomes the VLAN30/bbh-adversarial-tier relay specifically. Real rename
        pending (s1-relay-vlan30 or similar — exact hostname/role string not yet chosen).",
    concrete_step_2_deferred: "Whether clima/econ's future VLAN(s) get their OWN dedicated relay LXC, or stay
        on the cheaper host-level static-iptables containment pattern (vsf-vlan-policy.service class), is a
        genuine open question proportional to how much ACTIVE decision-making (vs static drop rules) their
        tier needs. Not decided — flagged for the same future design session as decision_1's open question.",
    function: "The relay is the literal containment chokepoint for its tier: on suspected compromise of any
        S1 behind it, the relay can cut that S1's traffic before corruption propagates past the tier boundary
        — an operational instantiation of algedonic containment at the network layer, not just detection.",
  },

  // ── DECISION 3: config-canon consolidation — single source + generators ───────────
  decision_3_config_canon: {
    trigger: "Found live today: state.db lxcs row for 101 said ip=10.10.20.101 (canon), real ip (pct config
        + ARP) is 10.10.20.10 — same bug class already fixed once for 107 (2026-06-06). Root cause is
        structural, not a one-off typo: network/firewall config is scattered across state.db, per-LXC .fw
        files, /etc/vsf/coupling/*.coupling.json (host, not in git), iptables rules embedded in systemd
        ExecStart= lines (vsf-nat-vlans/vsf-vlan-policy/vsf-soc-access services), /etc/network/interfaces,
        and CLAUDE.md's hand-maintained LXC table — each an independently-maintained artifact with nothing
        enforcing sync except topology_reconcile's SHA/process census (which does not check IP/rule
        correctness).",
    ratified_direction: "rmw3 proposed env-vars-on-n02 or a heavier RDBMS (MySQL) and asked for Claude's
        opinion explicitly, flagging it as speculation ('estoy especulando'). Claude's counter-proposal,
        ACCEPTED without amendment ('me parece bien tu propuesta, es algo programático y se resuelve con la
        arquitectura que tenemos'): reject both suggested mechanisms. Env vars would scatter the problem
        further (no relational/query capability). A new RDBMS doesn't fix the actual defect (state.db is
        already relationally capable; the defect is that it is DESCRIPTIVE not GENERATIVE — nothing writes
        the derived artifacts FROM it). Fix: keep state.db (extend schema as needed) as the single declarative
        source; build generators that WRITE the derived artifacts (.fw files, the iptables lines currently
        hand-embedded in systemd units, /etc/network/interfaces VLAN stanzas, CLAUDE.md's LXC table) FROM
        state.db, rather than hand-maintaining each separately. Same principle already applied elsewhere in
        this project (feedback_docs_index: 'never hand-edit INDEX, docs_reconcile.py --update --sync-db') —
        this decision is that principle extended to network/firewall config specifically, not a new one.",
    explicitly_rejected: "MySQL/Postgres migration — not because it's wrong at some future scale, but because
        at CURRENT scale it adds operational surface (its own service, backup, network ACL for the DB itself)
        without fixing the actual defect, which is architecture-of-sync, not engine power. Revisit only if a
        real need for concurrent multi-writer transactions across distributed agents appears — not now.",
  },

  // ── DECISION 4: resource-pool scope — TIER 3 study (disk + network) ───────────────
  decision_4_resource_pool_scope: {
    important_correction: "This question ('should ALL organelos enter the resource pool?') was ALREADY asked
        and ANSWERED: docs/spec/dynamic_resources_all_lxc.spec.vsm, ratified 2026-07-03. Claude's answer
        today (a floor/guaranteed-minimum tier for S2-S5 'regulatory' organelos + a dynamic proportional
        bargain tier for S1 'operational' units only) independently re-derived the SAME two-tier split
        already on record: TIER 1 = proportional bargain, S1+nucleus only (Beer-correct: S3 bargains with
        S1, not with its own regulatory layers). TIER 2 = defensive pressure-guard (mem/cpu floor+ceiling,
        OOM-guard semantics, NOT a market), covering ALL LXCs including S2-S5. rmw3 ratified TIER 2's exact
        params 2026-07-03 (guard_pool=1024M, GUARD_HI=90%mem×3ticks→+256M, GUARD_LO=60%×5ticks→revert,
        cap=+512M/LXC). STATUS PER THAT DOC: 'RATIFIED — right-sizing applied; TIER-2 guard params set,
        impl pending.' — i.e. TIER 2 was never actually built. This is a real, still-open task, older than
        anything else in this document, and should be folded into whatever gets dispatched next alongside
        decisions 1-3, not treated as newly settled today.",
    genuinely_new_today: "TIER 1/2 (ratified 07-03) only ever covered CPU/RAM. rmw3 today asked specifically
        about extending dynamic management to PHYSICAL DISK and eventually NETWORK LOAD — this is new scope,
        not covered by the existing spec. Motivating precedent is not hypothetical: memory
        project_algedonic_storage_blindspot — LVM-thin near-full on 101/112 stalled the S5 algedonic ledger
        8h silently, a real incident, not a speculative risk.",
    design_note: "CPU/RAM share one actuator class (Proxmox cgroup resize, atomic, reversible). Disk and
        network do not — LVM-thin resize is slower/less trivially reversible than a cgroup.max write, and
        bandwidth shaping is a structurally different mechanism (tc/qdisc or cgroup v2 io/net_cls controllers).
        Recommend: design as separate actuator adapters behind a SHARED grant-decision policy (the 'when to
        grant more' logic can stay common; 'how to execute it' must be resource-type-specific). rmw3 agreed
        ('me parece el angulo que propones') and asked to further study which OTHER resources beyond
        disk/network might warrant the same treatment — left open, not enumerated yet.",
  },

  // ── SIDE FINDING: the NATS crash-loop does not cleanly match the known bug class ──
  side_finding_nats_crashloop_not_yet_diagnosed: {
    what: "vsf-s3-alloc (LXC 101) found crash-looping against NATS connect (1104 restarts, TimeoutError,
        earliest retained log entry Jul 31 23:34 — predates this session). Checked against
        docs/spec/nats_auth_remediation/spec.vsm's already-diagnosed bug class ('hardcoded NATS_URL bypasses
        the token, connects at TCP fine then gets cleanly auth-rejected at the protocol layer, systemd stays
        active because it doesn't crash') — does NOT match: s3-alloc's NATS_URL already correctly includes
        the token (verified in /etc/default/vsf-s3-alloc) and already reads it from env (the fix pattern that
        spec recommends), yet still fails. The failure mode observed is a raw TCP-level connect() timeout
        inside the client library (not a post-connect auth rejection), and was INTERMITTENT under direct
        retest (1 success, 2 immediate failures) rather than the known bug's clean deterministic rejection.
        Firewall (106.fw correctly allow-lists 101's real IP) and token content were both ruled out live.",
    do_not_assume: "Whoever picks this up should NOT assume 'apply the July NATS remediation fix' resolves
        it — that fix targeted a different, already-understood failure signature. This needs fresh live
        diagnosis (check nats-server's own connz/rate-limit state on 106, check whether 1104 rapid
        reconnects self-inflicted a throttle) before any patch.",
    also_pending: "docs/tasks STATE-DB-101-IP-CANON-MISMATCH (state.db ip=10.10.20.101 vs real .10) — small,
        mechanical, zero live-traffic impact, blocked only on a state.db write-lock clearing (transient,
        was locked by a concurrent writer at time of attempted fix 2026-08-02T01:12 UTC).",
  },

  gate: "ALL of decisions 1-4 + the side finding stay QUEUED behind
      SOC-STAGE-A-PHASE1-WAZUH-MINIMAL-INSTALL (DS, in progress). rmw3 explicit: wait for DS to finish that
      task before dispatching any of this arc. When DS frees up, this doc is the dispatch-ready summary —
      no further design conversation needed before writing the actual directive, only sequencing/ordering
      across decisions 1-4 + the crash-loop diagnosis.",
}
